  "id": "hip-flexion",
  "name": "Hip Flexion Isolation",
  "definition": "Unilateral open-chain hip-flexion isolation with the working knee held straight. The supine table-supported bodyweight branch retains its active end range and nonstandardized pelvic/spinal motion from Okubo. The standing cable branch selects the supported straight-leg fixture in Speediance's technical instructions: low pulley behind, ankle cuff, upright controlled trunk and pelvis, and a thigh-horizontal or earlier form-limited endpoint. Iliopsoas is primary; rectus femoris and tensor fasciae latae are secondary. Yamane measured TFL during held supine straight-leg raises; transfer to these dynamic fixtures is an explicit categorical anatomy-and-mechanics inference, not exact-protocol EMG or a recruitment ranking. The iliopsoas region combines psoas major and iliacus. Abs and obliques control the trunk/pelvis; gluteus medius receives standing-branch pelvic-control credit. Sartorius and adductors remain outside this focused role expansion. Bent-knee marches, hanging or captain-chair raises, sit-ups, V-ups, bands, machines, unsupported cable raises, and deliberately spinal or pelvic driven lifts remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "okubo-2021-end-range-active-straight-leg-raise",
    "yamane-2019-straight-leg-raise",
    "speediance-2026-standing-cable-hip-flexion"
  ],
